<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lithium-ion-battery">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithium-ion battery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Li-ion battery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lithium-ion battery is a rechargeable battery that stores energy by moving lithium ions in and out of electronically conducting solids. M. Stanley Whittingham conceived intercalation electrodes in the 1970s and made the first rechargeable lithium-ion battery, with a titanium disulfide cathode, though safety problems kept it from being commercialized. John Goodenough used lithium cobalt oxide as a cathode in 1980, and Akira Yoshino developed the first prototype of the modern battery, with a carbonaceous anode, in 1985. A Sony and Asahi Kasei team led by Yoshio Nishi brought it to market in 1991, and the three inventors shared the 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Chemistry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithium-ion-battery.jpg" alt="Lithium-ion battery" width="330" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RudolfSimon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithium-Ion_Cell_cylindric.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>One of the earliest examples of research into lithium-ion batteries was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> fluoride and lithium battery developed by NASA in 1965. The breakthrough toward the modern battery came in 1974, when the British chemist M. Stanley Whittingham first used titanium disulfide as a cathode material. Its layered structure takes in lithium ions without significant change to the crystal.</p><p>Exxon tried to commercialise the battery in the late 1970s but found the synthesis expensive and complex, since titanium disulfide is sensitive to moisture and gives off toxic hydrogen sulfide on contact with water. The cells were also prone to catch fire spontaneously because of the metallic lithium in them, and Exxon discontinued development. The later prototype of Akira Yoshino replaced lithium metal with a carbonaceous anode.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A lithium-ion cell has an anode, a cathode, a separator, a casing, an electrolyte and a pair of current collectors. The negative electrode is typically graphite and the positive electrode a metal oxide or phosphate, while the electrolyte is a lithium salt in an organic solvent and the separator keeps the electrodes from shorting. In discharge, lithium ions carry the current through the electrolyte from the negative to the positive electrode. In charging, an external voltage forces them back to the negative electrode, where they become embedded in the porous material in a process called intercalation. Cells have a nominal voltage of 3.6 or 3.7 volts.</p><p>Different cathode materials suit different uses. Handheld electronics mostly use lithium cobalt oxide with a graphite anode, which offers high energy density, while lithium iron phosphate and lithium nickel manganese cobalt oxide may offer longer life and a higher discharge rate, and the latter is widely used in the electrification of transport.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Lithium-ion batteries generally have higher specific energy, energy density and efficiency, and a longer life, than other rechargeable types. In the three decades after they were first sold in 1991, their volumetric energy density increased threefold while their cost dropped tenfold. They have enabled portable consumer electronics, laptop computers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobile-phone">mobile phones</a> and electric cars, and they are used for grid-scale energy storage and in military and aerospace applications.</p><p>They carry a risk of fire or explosion because they contain flammable electrolytes, and overcharging can cause an explosion. Mining lithium is water-intensive, often in arid regions, and cobalt can be a conflict mineral, which has encouraged work on lithium iron phosphate and on sodium-ion and iron-air batteries. The 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Chemistry recognised the development of the battery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithium-ion battery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
